# Shell scripts are LF on every platform, including a Windows checkout.
#
# This file's absence made all three Windows CI legs red for eight days, across
# the v1.0.0 release commit. The repository's shell scripts were LF-clean the
# whole time; actions/checkout applied the Windows runner's core.autocrlf=true
# and rewrote them to CRLF on checkout, and shellcheck correctly refused to
# parse the result -- a here-document terminator with a trailing CR does not
# match its opener, so SC1017 cascaded into SC1073/SC1041/SC1042/SC1072 in
# install.sh and the gate's shellcheck step failed.
#
# **Deliberately surgical.** The obvious thing to write here is a repo-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf`, and it is deliberately NOT written: every other file
# type is currently converted by core.autocrlf on Windows and the suite is green
# over that, so a blanket rule would change the line endings of .py, .md, .json
# and .ps1 on the one platform that is currently failing. That is a second
# variable introduced into the middle of a release. Only the file type that is
# actually broken is pinned; anything else stays exactly as it behaves today.
#
# Ratcheted by tests/test_line_endings.py, which reads the git INDEX rather than
# the working tree -- on Linux a working-tree check passes vacuously and would
# have stayed green throughout the eight days CI was red.
#
# See specs/ci-green-on-windows.md.

*.sh text eol=lf
